  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon 500mb model height analysis and GOES satellite \nwater vapor imagery showed ridging over the south-central U.S., \ncentered over SE TX with the northern extent of the ridge up through \nthe Great Lakes and into western Quebec. A shortwave was moving \nthrough northern MN spreading cirrus clouds into the western U.P. \nWinds were light from the southeast and temperatures had recovered \nquite well, into the 60s, for much of the U.P. Scattered fair \nweather cumulus had formed with daytime heating over the western \nU.P. and also over the east, near Lake Michigan.\n\nRidging will be in place through the weekend over the south-central \nU.S. and will continue to extend northwards into the Upper Great \nLakes, with an omega like blocking pattern tonight through Saturday. \nLREF ensembles are showing a rapid weaking in 500mb positive height \nanomalies early next week with troughing developing over central \nCanada and into the central U.S. This will bring a shift from the \nwarm, dry weather to cooler weather along with the chance for \nprecipitation next Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThrough the weekend, the blocking ridge and an extensive surface \nhigh pressure will continue to bring dry weather with slightly above \nnormal daytime temperatures (highs in the 60s to low 70s) and \nseasonably cool nights (lows in the 40s, with upper 30s over the \ninterior). Winds will also be light with a weak pressure gradient \nover the region. A few disturbances will ride the western edge of \nthe upper level ridge bringing precipitation close to the U.P. at \ntimes but likely remaining west of the area. A period of cloudiness \ncan be expected tonight as the first disturbance pushes north \nthrough MN. Skies will clear again during the afternoon Friday with \nadditional cloudiness expected over the far west for the weekend, \nwhile the central and east remain under clear to partly cloudy \nskies. Fog formation is less likely tonight as high clouds should \nkeep temperatures from bottoming out as much as the previous nights.\n\nAdditional shortwaves will move around the western edge of the ridge \nthis weekend bringing clouds into the far west. However, expecting \nthe deeper moisture to stay west of the U.P. with only and outside \nchance (<15%) for light showers to impact the far west Saturday \nnight.\n\nLate in the extended forecast, Tuesday into Wednesday, probabilities \nfor more appreciable precipitation begin to increase. LREF is \nindicating a 50% chance for 0.5\" or more of rainfall in the far \nwest, with lesser chances (30%) in the far east. There is still a \nlot of uncertainty with the evolution of the system including the \ntrack and strength, with drier solutions showing a low or open wave \ntracking north of Lake Superior with shorter duration precipitation \nas the cold front swings through the U.P. Wetter solutions show a \nstronger surface low tracking across the U.P. but these wetter \nsolution are outliers at this time.",
